# Allow the meshpoint service user to invoke lxmsendmsg as the user
# that owns the LXMF identity (typically `mp`), without a password.
#
# This is the narrowest possible grant that makes the Meshpoint
# dashboard's "send LXMF message" endpoint work:
#   - only one specific binary (lxmsendmsg) -- no shell, no other
#     commands
#   - only one target user (the placeholder __USER__ is substituted
#     by setup_rnsd.sh at install time, e.g. mp)
#   - NOPASSWD so the FastAPI request handler doesn't hang on a
#     terminal that doesn't exist
#
# Installed at /etc/sudoers.d/meshpoint-lxmf with mode 0440 (the
# permission sudo demands for drop-in files). setup_rnsd.sh validates
# the rendered file with `visudo -cf` before moving it into place --
# a syntactically-broken sudoers file would lock the box out of sudo
# entirely, which we never want to risk.
#
# Tightening considered + rejected:
#   - Restricting arguments (e.g. only allow hex hashes) -- sudoers
#     argument matching is fiddly and easy to get wrong; the request
#     validation lives in the FastAPI handler instead.
#   - Granting via a setuid wrapper script -- harder to audit,
#     harder to revoke, and sudoers is the standard pattern for
#     this kind of cross-user delegation on Linux.
meshpoint ALL=(__USER__) NOPASSWD: __LXMSENDMSG__, __LXMANNOUNCE__
# Phase 1 #6b: "Restart Stack" button on the Radio tab status card
# triggers `sudo systemctl restart rnsd.service`. lxmd cascades via
# its PartOf=rnsd.service declaration so we only need the one
# command, not separate rules for both services.
meshpoint ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl restart rnsd.service
